§§ `Section 548 -- Order to stand trial or discharge`

-- 548 (1) When all the evidence has been taken by the justice, he shall (a) if in his opinion there is sufficient evidence to put the accused on trial for the offence charged or any other indictable offence in respect of the same transaction, order the accused to stand trial;
-- or (b) discharge the accused, if in his opinion on the whole of the evidence no sufficient case is made out to put the accused on trial for the offence charged or any other indictable offence in respect of the same transaction.
-- The answer is the decision (the core's `Decision on the charge`: an order to stand trial, or a discharge at the preliminary inquiry); NOTHING before all the evidence has been taken. (The seam name other parts ask is `s 548 the decision the justice made at the preliminary inquiry`.)
-- src:12103 | 548 (1) When all the evidence has been taken by the justice, he shall
-- src:12104 | (a) if in his opinion there is sufficient evidence to put the accused on trial for the offence charged or any other indictable offence in respect of the same transaction, order the accused to stand trial; or
-- src:12105 | (b) discharge the accused, if in his opinion on the whole of the evidence no sufficient case is made out to put the accused on trial for the offence charged or any other indictable offence in respect of the same transaction.
